in  part to the efforts of these creators  three authors all of whom are deeply  involved in the paleontology community  themselves  make it clear that modern  paleontologists aren't just blindly  interpreting fossils but also making  great strides in integrating studies of  feather impressions and soft tissue  preservation  to gain more complete pictures of what  these incredible creatures might have  looked like  however like with any science there is  still of course a diversity of  fascinating unknowns when it comes to  dinosaurs  and all yesterday's explores how  speculation as long as it's cautiously  grounded in science can both help  and even play a vital role in showcasing  the intriguing possibilities of how  dinosaurs might have looked and behave  all yesterday's stress is a balance  between caution and speculation  always pointing out that many of the  reconstructions within the pages  might be completely off the mark that's  just the nature of speculation with that  said  let's get started and take a closer look  at what unique aspects of dinosaurs may  not show up in the fossil record  the first out of the norm reconstruction  of a dinosaur all yesterday's explorers  is of a carnotaurus  drawn at a unique angle by john conway  while the bullheaded predators are  usually depicted from the side  here the authors depict the animal  head-on drawing attention to just how  strange the animal looks  with his arms stuck out to the side in a  bizarre display the idea behind this  strange spectacle comes from the animal  skeleton which shows the animal had  stunted  ineffectual arms yet possessed unique  shoulders which would have allowed the  animals to spread these arms out  sideways in a way utterly dissimilar to  other  large predatory dinosaurs an ability  which seems to serve no  practical purpose so the authors imagine  the arms on the carnotaurus and the  related majungasaurus  pictured here in cm cozman's  one-of-a-kind style were waved about  in mating or territorial displays  potentially alongside bright colors  not unlike many birds today a bizarre  concept for sure  but not altogether improbable given the  available paleontological data  of course some dinosaur colors might  have also gone in the opposite direction  can you see the majungasaurus in this  image how about now  here the authors explore the possibility  that some dinosaurs might have had  appearances which supported camouflage  in mimicry  blending into the natural features of  the environment much like a modern stick  bug  now it's usually smaller animals not  predators like majungasaurus which  employ camouflage  but some large modern predators body  patterns like the tiger's distinctive  stripes which allow them to blend into  tall grasses do help them  ambush prey so it's certainly possible  some dinosaurs might have had  similar camouflaged appearances here's  another recreation which  at first might be difficult to see this  is all yesterday's speculative concept  for police leasoria portraying the  animal with impressive camouflage  strange though this might seem to anyone  familiar with traditional portrayals of  plesiosauria  the modern oceans are swarming with  remarkable forms of mimicry  just look at the incredible appearance  of something like a leafy sea dragon  and you'll start to understand just how  amazing past ocean life might have  looked  now the authors aren't actually making a  scientific assertion that this is what  all plesiosauria which for the record  weren't actually dinosaurs but adjacent  marine reptiles  looked like most please the asuria  likely had smooth skin  for better hydrodynamics but it's not  impossible some branches tried an ambush  approach  which may have favored better camouflage  an interesting possibility for sure  back on land here's a quite heavily  feathered speculative reinvention  of a therizinosaurus the therizinosaurus  looked quite menacing based on its  skeleton alone  thanks to the animal's absurdly long  claws which in the past excited artists  and caused them to make overly deadly  looking animals despite the fact the  animal wasn't a predator the thick layer  of feathers certainly makes the animal  look  less frightening and quite different  from their skeletal appearance  if you compare the gaunt skeleton of say  an owl however to the quite different  shape of the actual bird  you can start to see how such a  difference between skeleton and body  size  isn't too crazy a concept  therazinosaurus skeletons also have wide  hips  so it's been theorized they have sizable  physiques  beyond protofeathers all yesterdays also  explores the hypothetical that some  dinosaurs  like heterodontosaurus might have had  defensive quill-like structures not  unlike the defense of the modern  porcupine  seems hard to believe well a recently  exceptionally well-preserved specimen of  the early ceratopsian statakosaurus was  found with bristle-like quills on its  tail  the sitakasaurus is actually one of the  most completely reconstructed dinosaurs  and its appearance is  certainly not tame paleontologists don't  think those strange bristles on its tail  were for self-defense but it's certainly  possible that some genuses  like heterodontosaurus which we also now  know had some kind of spike like dermal  quills  could have used their bristles for  self-defense going even further into  hypothetical territory regarding  proto-feathers the authors imagine a  group of lielen asoria amicographica as  round furballs  quite different from the popular notion  of a dinosaur  far from fanciful speculation however  the author's recreation is based on  evidence that the lielan asoria lived in  a near polar region  and almost certainly had to endure  sub-zero temperatures  while one might look at such a  reconstruction and think that looks too  adorable for a dinosaur  the authors point out that the hesitancy  around depicting dinosaurs as  cute is potentially an overly limiting  one also incorporated into this design  liel and asoria tales were almost three  times as long as their body  for reasons paleontologists aren't quite  certain of  all yesterdays depicts their tales as  strange flagpole like tools for social  displays while easy to dismiss this  concept is overly speculative  the authors point out that while it's  certainly unlikely it's only so much  weirder than what you might see  anywhere else in nature but appearance  is only one half of the speculative  puzzle when it comes to dinosaurs  the authors observe that various unique  behaviors likely weren't preserved  within the fossil record  just think about the mating dances of  birds of paradise the unique halls of  whales or even something as simple as  the dung rolling of dung beetles and  you'll start to realize that behaviors  which give animals huge parts of their  identity  can often not be understood from remains  alone  and it stands to reason some dinosaurs  might have had similarly bizarre  behaviors which fall into the same  category  here's an intriguing speculative example  of elasmosaurus marine reptiles which  also aren't actually dinosaurs  behaving in a highly unusual manner here  the authors invent a scenario where the  elasmosaurus  lunge out of the depth and wave their  necks competing to prove who is the  strongest  older art of elasmosaurus sometimes  shows them holding their necks above the  water's surface with ease  but modern paleontology has proven these  animals had heavy bones and denser necks  which could only be lifted out of the  water with significant effort  so the author imagines these dinosaurs  exerting such a strain as a form of  social competition  of course this is absolutely and  unapologetically speculative yet  equivalently remarkable behavior is  likely lost  somewhere in our past in a similar vein  of speculative thought  here the authors imagine protoceratops  engaging in a tree-climbing behavior  there's no direct evidence for in the  slightest  and yet certain breeds of goats also  climb trees  a strange-looking behavior you wouldn't  guess the hoofed mammals would engage in  based on their body plans alone  and yes these modern goats they are not  a hoax they truly hang out in trees  it's something like this sort of  unexpected contact which might be absent  from the fossil record  on the subject of dinosaurs doing things  they're not known for  playing is certainly a significant  example instead of showing a dinosaur  striking a pose or getting attacked by a  predator  this recreation shows a chimerasaurus  enjoying a  simple role in the mud speculative  though this may seem  almost all living animals engage in some  form of play  both larger mammals but also various  species of birds reptiles and even fish  most animals have been observed taking  part in behavior for no practical  reason other than it seems to be fun so  dinosaurs even if they're sometimes  thought of creatures that lived in  constant battle with one another  almost certainly engaged in playful  behavior too  and here at the end is an image which  despite its simplicity  is one of the hardest to process it  shows a t-rex  just sleeping in a peaceful field but so  much of a classic perception of the  creature is of a roaring monster with  its jaw as a gate  ready to strike that we forget the  tyrannosaurus is also  just an animal and animals sleep  especially hunting animals who need to  conserve energy and therefore spend a  considerable amount of time  resting it's not certain if t-rexes  slept on their sides or not or if they  had this particular coloration  but it's still an intriguing image that  gets the viewer to think of the animal  as more than just a machine of death  ultimately science and speculation  aren't concepts in opposition to each  other  all paleontological art of long-extinct  creatures  is speculative to some degree and  thinking about interesting albeit  unusual possibilities can help us  appreciate all we know  and don't know about these remarkable  animals from the distant  past and that's the end of our glimpse  at the intriguing all yesterdays  if you like this content please consider  supporting cosmin nation conway's work  by purchasing a copy of the book  and by patronizing their other works and  social media which as always you can  find in the links below  also thanks for watching if you enjoyed  this entry please lend your support  and like subscribe and hit the  notification icon to stay up to date on  all things curious  see you in the next video  you

